The block defines circle functions and states the accepted Euclidean rotation, arc and sector-area premises. Matrix composition derives addition identities; symmetry and periodicity lead to amplitude/phase and restricted inverse domains. The geometric squeeze proves the small-angle limits before deriving both trigonometric derivatives, chain factors, second derivatives, integrals and a full-cycle mean square. Zero amplitude and zero angular frequency receive separate treatment. Complex numbers are constructed as ordered pairs with explicit operations, conjugation, norm and inverse; multiplication, integer powers and all roots of a nonzero complex number follow from rotation and polar form. The unit-circle notation is not silently identified with a complex exponential: Euler's identity and full complex analysis remain deferred.

For positive ω, the initial-value equation x''+ω²x=0 has the verified solution x=x₀ cos(ωt)+(v₀/ω) sin(ωt). The constant nonnegative quadratic of the difference proves uniqueness without invoking an undeveloped general theorem. The zero-frequency solution is x₀+v₀t. Endpoint data give a unique solution when sin(ωL) is nonzero; at its zeros, compatible data admit infinitely many solutions and incompatible data admit none. A finite Taylor integral remainder is derived from integration by parts, bounding the degree-59 trigonometric reference by |x|⁶⁰/60!. No infinite-series convergence theorem or physical oscillator experiment is claimed.

The original study multiplies the rational unit point (3/5,4/5) by (2,−1), obtaining (2,1), and verifies its squared rotation (−7/25,24/25). Initial data x₀=3, v₀=8, ω=2 give x=3 cos(2t)+4 sin(2t), amplitude 5, invariant 100 and full-cycle mean square 25/2. Two different functions, 3 cos(πt/2)+4 sin(πt/2) and 3 cos(5πt/2)+4 sin(5πt/2), agree at every integer time but give opposite values ±7/√2 at t=1/2 and different initial derivatives. This is an explicit sampling ambiguity with a proof, not a full sampling theorem.

